---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import VideoRemux from '@/components/tools/media/VideoRemux.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="webm-to-mp4">
  <VideoRemux
    client:load
    from="webm"
    target="mp4"
    hint="Screen recordings and Discord clips, converted on your device"
  />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Why WebM to MP4 needs a full re-encode</h2>
    <p>
      Most container conversions on this site are a copy: the compressed data already fits the destination
      and only the wrapper changes. WebM is different. It holds VP8 or VP9 video and Opus audio, which MP4
      players do not support. Each frame must be decoded to pixels and re-encoded as H.264, and the audio
      re-encoded as AAC. There is no shortcut: a WebM file that is only renamed to .mp4 fails on the device
      that needs MP4.
    </p>
    <p>
      The encode runs on your processor, so there is no queue, size limit or watermark, and no copy of your
      screen recording stays on a server.
    </p>

    <h2>How long the conversion takes</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Clip length</th>
            <th>720p screen recording</th>
            <th>1080p camera footage</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>30 seconds</td>
            <td>15-40 seconds</td>
            <td>30-60 seconds</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>5 minutes</td>
            <td>3-7 minutes</td>
            <td>5-12 minutes</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>30 minutes</td>
            <td>20-45 minutes</td>
            <td>30 minutes to well over an hour</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      These are typical laptop times. Two settings make it much faster: the <strong>small file</strong> quality
      preset, and a faster encoder preset under advanced options. Screen recordings compress very well because
      most of the frame does not change between frames, so the faster settings cost little visible quality.
    </p>

    <h2>The conversion also fixes seeking</h2>
    <p>
      A recording made in a browser, by the <a href="/screen-recorder">screen recorder</a> on this site, by a
      web recording app or by a WebRTC call recorder, comes out of the MediaRecorder API with no duration in
      the header. The file is complete and plays from the start, but the player does not know its length, so
      the scrub bar is useless and you cannot drag it. Re-encoding writes a correct header, and the timeline
      works again. Convert a browser recording before you send it.
    </p>

    <h2>Where WebM files come from</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Streaming and recording software set to WebM output.</li>
      <li>Discord clips and downloads.</li>
      <li>Browser screen and camera recorders, which record WebM natively.</li>
      <li>Saved web video: YouTube and many other sites send VP9 in WebM to browsers that support it.</li>
      <li>Telegram and WhatsApp stickers, which are short VP9 WebM animations.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Audio quality</h2>
    <p>
      Opus is a better codec than AAC at the same bitrate, so re-encoding costs a little quality in principle.
      At the 160 kbps AAC this page writes, the difference is inaudible on speech and typical music. If the
      result is silent, the source had no audio track. The usual cause is a screen recording started without
      the “share audio” option, and the audio cannot be recovered later.
    </p>

    <h2>The reverse direction</h2>
    <p>
      For a smaller file for a website, use <a href="/mp4-to-webm">MP4 to WebM</a>. If you only need the sound,
      <a href="/webm-to-mp3">WebM to MP3</a> skips the video work and finishes much faster.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
